}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434269} {:answer/id #uuid "9428f7fa-4636-5ad9-ac5c-92e2a7fbe6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9428f7fa-4636-5ad9-ac5c-92e2a7fbe6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434270} :answer-field/id #uuid "29540716-87b2-5957-a8c4-7141164fdefb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434273}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5fbef9b7-68b9-5b6d-beff-b38a63574b1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-16458" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2adb84964a605495c84921943ec76f9820ba18851cda817037dd6022808d1fd7.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087517 :question/id #uuid "b7c94673-f847-5977-9b9f-8d235ea42eb2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5073" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation $\\dfrac{x^2}{36} + \\dfrac{y^2}{25} = 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $a^2=36$ and $b^2=25.$\n\n- Since $a^2=36,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{36} = 6.$\n\n- Since $b^2=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25} = 5.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=5.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a = 2 \\cdot 6 = 12,$ and the minor axis has length $2b = 2 \\cdot 5 =10.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e43e393-8752-58bc-b87c-b72f679cbfd9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b14229b-7244-5a13-b9f7-377caf1b3763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342721} {:answer/id #uuid "5f7b224c-d2f2-57f2-923d-636856ca9541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343046} {:answer/id #uuid "909cdc5c-8c31-567d-a8db-9603fcfc60fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343502} {:answer/id #uuid "abc3321f-0ab1-56d9-b892-997b85188d6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343749} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb1fa09-1293-5ba1-af7a-c2963a194695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f7b224c-d2f2-57f2-923d-636856ca9541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343046} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3d2e838-6d36-58ab-9502-f89e82764f33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344893}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b586f348-117c-513c-b7f9-86528f54bc6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-7598" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=4$ and ${b}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=4,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e887380e-c6a2-55c5-a953-644c684c42f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086587} {:answer/id #uuid "022e8e85-f2f9-5f22-89c3-32299f61eba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{10}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086588} {:answer/id #uuid "25b8aed4-093a-5a57-ad29-a6f742a77db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086589} {:answer/id #uuid "37b46b49-d523-50f1-9995-b5a05ce4f820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{10}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086590} {:answer/id #uuid "60da5fd4-8377-57c4-87c2-582cca3b9c69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086591}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dc165d99-699e-586e-b731-222ff1843dbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086586}] :question/id #uuid "256fdb5e-1d5d-5f2a-8bad-6f30d3b32832" :question/math-academy-id "q-7600" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation\n$\\frac{x^{2}}{10} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9} = 1$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a537988-fe3f-54c0-9969-840ebacfdc49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$16$ and $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247941} {:answer/id #uuid "47dcabf5-4c17-538f-b234-536fc0e79fb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248066} {:answer/id #uuid "84dca1dd-ac85-5c5e-981b-8c8116385a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $3.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248603} {:answer/id #uuid "a90d8381-a4e7-5c6c-b202-bb25fb3dee30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248927} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe41db8-dff4-598f-8f14-5e33cb612563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $2\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffe41db8-dff4-598f-8f14-5e33cb612563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $2\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249664} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb465769-f76a-5b09-871d-27ae5361defb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250021}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0af912c0-685f-5b27-b24c-c0de6bcff1d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-115132" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=7.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=7,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{7}=2\\sqrt{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab7bfc4-fe0b-5271-b8ca-448cc2f51cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ and $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247805} {:answer/id #uuid "515c4642-4ca5-5554-a9a2-a99042231e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $2.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248154} {:answer/id #uuid "5390bb0d-24f6-53b5-94f1-003ff942f21a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$50$ and $12.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248176} {:answer/id #uuid "7c004586-c7f0-5ef1-b7fc-ca0dc8582a7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$100$ and $25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248512} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dfbabe-5660-549f-96d1-e202fb8340ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ab7bfc4-fe0b-5271-b8ca-448cc2f51cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ and $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247805} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ac28089-ed40-5056-81d0-446e067aefea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249880}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41a42db1-4c6e-506f-8c7d-8db6141e0014" :question/math-academy-id "q-115135" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=100$ and ${b}^{2}=25.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=100,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{100}=10.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=10,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=5.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 10=20,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 5=10.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22d9bb51-7d28-5203-a100-671bfb7b1984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281995} {:answer/id #uuid "a39c00a4-afcb-520f-b854-4bc1d26a2c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283115} {:answer/id #uuid "b2234da2-6880-5948-bede-cdef269e6b98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283232} {:answer/id #uuid "cbccc52e-55e7-5e83-85bf-7ec1fde391a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283470} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3590a4-fe8c-559b-a199-73736d3c5178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a39c00a4-afcb-520f-b854-4bc1d26a2c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283115} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a67a626-3555-5355-acc5-d65f47afb3ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284142}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc47bd40-4608-5f54-a026-2eadf6dc5b59" :question/math-academy-id "q-16452" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=4$ and ${b}^{2}=2.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=4,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=2,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=2,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ea4dfa-3746-512d-a4e5-fa19281f7cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281707} {:answer/id #uuid "119639f4-439c-5028-9a09-08e66ef5732b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281856} {:answer/id #uuid "376b6ab6-79ed-53f0-aaa3-87bd75aa6c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282182} {:answer/id #uuid "66196dd8-4f71-57e1-8517-24340f5f07aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{3}$ and $2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282569} {:answer/id #uuid "95c0623c-a78b-5fec-8c1e-678b85527dbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66196dd8-4f71-57e1-8517-24340f5f07aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{3}$ and $2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282569} :answer-field/id #uuid "29260e1c-0c08-5549-8699-d160c488888a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284008}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a341dec-953c-5c37-b7a1-a5cd95c6de7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-16454" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=3$ and ${b}^{2}=12.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=3,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=12,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{3},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177cbba2-b79a-5f5d-9c48-f99cc18456dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "177cbba2-b79a-5f5d-9c48-f99cc18456dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293025} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b444896-e044-5921-b7f7-0da6232a651c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294016} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89cbf232-7d5e-5370-98f0-434b0ca4424c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89cbf232-7d5e-5370-98f0-434b0ca4424c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293468} :answer-field/id #uuid "643cdb73-3055-524d-9fe7-30a337996809" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294284}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b7e4e8f-cd81-5ee3-ac55-a56d0433ac6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-277923" :question/problem "For the ellipse \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=64$ and ${b}^{2}=81.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=64,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{64}=8.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=81,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{81}=9.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=9,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=8.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 9=18,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 8=16.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dc25ece-e5e8-5341-b369-60c89d7b7cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6dc25ece-e5e8-5341-b369-60c89d7b7cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293391} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b5ea0c3-f3db-587a-a55c-d246902036fa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294196} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8edd52d8-a856-5699-9135-823330f651e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8edd52d8-a856-5699-9135-823330f651e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293486} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2a02e34-a1c4-5302-b6ca-ab014cebd574" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294782}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26bde4a4-e1ca-5778-ae0f-4fa44e3f19d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-279165" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=9$ and ${b}^{2}=4.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=9,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=4,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=3,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=2.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 3=6,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c243753-ebfb-5bd5-b708-979771972883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id 