nswer/id #uuid "dc66f534-da0e-5146-9fee-e309f47a07d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$50$ and $32$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d72c0683-cce5-5c5b-b1ee-f4a799dd53cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344126} :answer-field/id #uuid "59484f16-7ef8-58ef-819d-3d6908e8ec4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344643}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "548834cd-5f39-5629-b131-aad9f4ac51b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-7601" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=25.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 5=10,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0b4d7d8-6b13-51b3-8c16-f1f796b0790a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0b4d7d8-6b13-51b3-8c16-f1f796b0790a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370022} :answer-field/id #uuid "1784709b-8c12-545a-96ba-dc167b20f7c0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370021} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e70a177c-e321-5d97-ad0f-2390d9f8f894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e70a177c-e321-5d97-ad0f-2390d9f8f894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370024} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c229291-398d-5b8b-8d76-841b2f6ef86c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370023}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a5d8fed-b754-51cf-933f-f6f47cc1ecb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-279198" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=8$ and ${b}^{2}=24.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=8,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=24,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{24}=2\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{6},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{6}=4\\sqrt{6},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396120 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2368068-c473-5919-aff6-53a14b246b5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396122} {:answer/id #uuid "801b88de-1112-5c33-bd1b-ddb864de2c6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396123} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd49079-798b-58f7-8490-cd6fb613a033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396124} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b72ba2-9df1-5acc-b385-173c4e36f9a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$18$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396125} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6ef04e-258f-5d68-a139-8ed8a39c2ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c6ef04e-258f-5d68-a139-8ed8a39c2ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396126} :answer-field/id #uuid "bef4ba21-7e9c-5949-8c09-0a45d37f8298" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396121}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "480a616c-8c80-5f81-b3d5-aa7488c0665b" :question/math-academy-id "q-7596" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{18}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=9$ and ${b}^{2}=18.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=9,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=18,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=3\\sqrt{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}=6\\sqrt{2},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 3=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcb6c4d2-fc5f-560f-9373-4f7a33529527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396129} {:answer/id #uuid "6565345d-7cca-5723-8a4a-06b781ec17a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396130} {:answer/id #uuid "71576bd8-08e5-58e9-999c-df3860cd2885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ and $16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396131} {:answer/id #uuid "b48bdeab-9afb-508b-a31f-840d2716535b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396132} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd4bf3f-6eb6-53a8-9729-d9955e874477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{5}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcb6c4d2-fc5f-560f-9373-4f7a33529527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396129} :answer-field/id #uuid "abca09ba-0b0a-5885-90fb-fd1d7d5af56c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396128}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e572cb8d-0721-59ec-897a-5e0fa685feb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-7597" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{20}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=20$ and ${b}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=20,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{5},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}=4\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 4=8.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4ffa0ff-df0b-5bc5-b896-3f6772a35019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$28$ and $24$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399852} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f1177f-3e11-593f-ad9c-dda3299fba50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$24.5$ and $18$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399853} {:answer/id #uuid "e59ee665-26a4-5d2e-8c55-26143f2480d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399854} {:answer/id #uuid "ee829b75-97f1-545e-8cf4-3704340f4ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$49$ and $36$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399855} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e390e1-9f85-5321-a954-3f147b97e82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e59ee665-26a4-5d2e-8c55-26143f2480d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399854} :answer-field/id #uuid "44102d96-20a4-51fc-add2-7a7a026df6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399851}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3113828e-6ca6-530b-804d-2cdc91aade73" :question/math-academy-id "q-115134" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=49$ and ${b}^{2}=36.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=49,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=36,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=6.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 6=12.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "755a881c-6b14-5e96-bd68-6af58d5ef813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401539} {:answer/id #uuid "5965e390-56a0-55ef-a7dc-eff34c09cc53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401540} {:answer/id #uuid "1f629da2-c549-5180-8903-3a78b8767616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401541} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1fc5c2-bdfa-52e9-a59e-c6227d171448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401542} {:answer/id #uuid "47a16fa9-9d49-55d0-a143-c2b6cca5c4dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f629da2-c549-5180-8903-3a78b8767616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401541} :answer-field/id #uuid "b41dd28d-0ca0-5222-9cfe-700ddaa37f17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401538}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e791f5b6-5e18-534a-99e5-45cc16c1809f" :question/math-academy-id "q-16455" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=1$ and ${b}^{2}=8.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=1,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{1}=1.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=8,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=1.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 1=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dcd62ed-d1d5-5d66-b9a7-d3752b72ffcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dcd62ed-d1d5-5d66-b9a7-d3752b72ffcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434263} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f59ee40-6a49-5f76-9b09-b56f1e7b8c65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434274} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb3f03d-cfef-5f9f-b396-26b221d4f856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cb3f03d-cfef-5f9f-b396-26b221d4f856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434266} :answer-field/id #uuid "62dc0b83-a470-59d2-b62b-b5ccbaa907c1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434275}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c46acbe-8af8-5805-a582-9ade9300c9a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-279199" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{13}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=13$ and ${b}^{2}=36.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=13,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{13}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=36,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{13}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{13}=2\\sqrt{13}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb06d992-e82f-5977-a714-2f2fd87616b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb06d992-e82f-5977-a714-2f2fd87616b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434271} :answer-field/id #uuid "13f8d6b7-9861-51ad-96d2-5cd3b96789b2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434272} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56513915-f193-5d63-936d-07a8b133ceae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56513915-f193-5d63-936d-07a8b133ceae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434268} :answer-field/id #uuid "878be2a7-5abd-5dc4-9fe0-30ce370e3435" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434276}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb8398f9-e97a-58eb-bd8b-b49ccd2d689d" :question/math-academy-id "q-279167" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=49$ and ${b}^{2}=25.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=49,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=5.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 5=10.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087543 :question/id #uuid "e429a65a-d65d-5b3f-a054-e3ab8a61caf3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5299" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2-x-6}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x .$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)(x-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x = -2$ and $x = 3.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x = -2$ and $x = 3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bf54f4a-6fe0-5828-91b7-9a1f23658966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2, x = 0, x = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085639} {:answer/id #uuid "bc557aaf-c556-5e9b-a479-3e86966eca1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2, x = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085640} {:answer/id #uuid "25d012a4-e043-5863-a60e-562fbc6d4763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2, x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085641} {:answer/id #uuid "49ed6d3a-0c88-59f1-bad1-a0f0e26881dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085642} {:answer/id #uuid "515ba722-7f0f-5fde-bdb4-11ae120a46ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 0, x = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085643}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9a893397-b983-54ae-b23f-7a638107daf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085638}] :question/id #uuid "9c5d95ad-bb5b-5734-95d6-fffbd7781121" :question/math-academy-id "q-39796" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{2}{2 - x} + \\frac{3}{2 + x}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3ecee87-11a8-5b31-9dca-3a44b18f83fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085759} {:answer/id #uuid "cd85c226-9ca4-5e6c-89fa-56a35f63ef57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 1, x = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085760} {:answer/id #uuid "296eff6c-9206-5823-bb93-0f28b35e920f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, x = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085761} {:answer/id #uuid "5b320f84-2893-5a99-b959-4cf809c16c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085762} {:answer/id #uuid "6e876cfd-ddd5-5cc2-a627-8112dec7ef0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085763}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7f48246f-51c4-5662-886c-8db6c571a5c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085758}] :question/id #uuid "8fc376c7-6855-5bb9-8474-018bf5ce2776" :question/math-academy-id "q-4629" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{3x^{2} - 12x}{x^{2} - 2x - 3}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30bf1417-e438-575a-b2ad-9a5df5d2c756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 3, x = \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086299} {:answer/id #uuid "34051aff-0352-5996-91d8-59092638e892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-12, x = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086300} {:answer/id #uuid "5179640e-a535-5308-8b8d-8f7ce13b179b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-5, x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086301} {:answer/id #uuid "5327d472-f83e-5dba-896d-945e7a11005c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2, x = \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086302} {:answer/id #uuid "7feffe94-3416-5a5f-9304-9e981d4f3453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-8, x = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086303}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8e7551a9-0422-54da-a740-462fe7d4546c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086298}] :question/id #uuid "300e7717-c412-5929-8eff-86d376ba1c7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6908" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{2} + 6x - 16}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48d343cf-bcd6-550a-b491-b3a8f24b897d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48d343cf-bcd6-550a-b491-b3a8f24b897d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232435} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a58a3d7-2ee5-5f1e-bb4b-407526cca168" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8c830c3-be7a-5e3a-ae63-2642c7c3a58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8c830c3-be7a-5e3a-ae63-2642c7c3a58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232437} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ec75490-1f9d-535a-8111-27b3e668b46e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232436}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5879d675-dcdb-5d51-8bbe-dbca569c522b" :question/math-academy-id "q-278249" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}(x-3)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(x-3)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=0$ and $x=3.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=0$ and $x=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234019 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34e39715-21d0-5ed5-8865-d1712b1e9cdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-39790" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{2{x}^{2}+x-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+1}{(2x+3)(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+3)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "549a35d4-daa7-5446-a638-9c14ec7a2e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "549a35d4-daa7-5446-a638-9c14ec7a2e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290993} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d774430-842a-541f-9061-499e5425b97e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291946} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c70ef40b-b3c8-5594-b1de-fc4a498090cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c70ef40b-b3c8-5594-b1de-fc4a498090cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291487} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ec5b0d-a086-5286-b7b0-f076712442f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292499}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "058c4194-dfaa-5b79-9c0a-c7395af3b9d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-269955" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{2}+9x}{{x}^{2}-4}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3x(2x+3)}{(x-2)(x+2)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e26fefe4-4552-5e54-8943-082eca6e26f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e26fefe4-4552-5e54-8943-082eca6e26f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293821} :answer-field/id #uuid "390c6ae0-8d81-5b0d-8148-b7b8e853b57c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294123} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca33217-9f32-559d-8ee5-0dd07420ba0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ca33217-9f32-559d-8ee5-0dd07420ba0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292986} :answer-field/id #uuid "e24f017a-5ffe-50af-8304-ccc74a86bcb5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294726}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cdefa676-1018-5cf9-a9ee-3c8d0b2dc9e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-278534" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2}{x-1}+\\frac{1}{x+5}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nAdding both fractions and then factoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2}{x-1}+\\frac{1}{x+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+5)+(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+10+x-1}{(x-1)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x+9}{(x-1)(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-5$ and $x=1.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-5$ and $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "680b28ce-e155-5b00-b8b0-99b62cf2c874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324839} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3410ea-fc43-5f14-adf6-d8f0c968b533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324873} {:answer/id #uuid "834d459f-93be-51d4-a7ef-620128a21142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325076} {:answer/id #uuid "d7774069-0d55-5431-b454-59b7537bb35f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=0,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325845} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf2746c-d5f4-5dd7-81ec-fe0ec7f75242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebf2746c-d5f4-5dd7-81ec-fe0ec7f75242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326048} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cf1b6f2-8390-50e9-8126-9a31d06519c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326298}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "347c5f9b-d82c-54af-b86f-728a645905de" :question/math-academy-id "q-39791" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}-8}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3}{2(x+2)(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252db0d7-6b72-5535-bc72-42087bd9c168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345686} {:answer/id #uuid "3c61c17f-df35-5231-8f8c-9855ef44fbff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345862} {:answer/id #uuid "493990c6-7871-5af7-9742-efe497570105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345976} {:answer/id #uuid "7aebd289-0443-5cd1-8647-ef80a5d4ed52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346424} {:answer/id #uuid "e86bd07f-57d6-5d65-989c-5c3ebae8b75b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aebd289-0443-5cd1-8647-ef80a5d4ed52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346424} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0a0cf42-b725-56a9-a31d-6affed13eb8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347968}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45255a84-5494-574a-bd9f-880b4b37a48c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80669" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}-3x-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x(x+3)}{(x+1)(x-4)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-1$ and $x=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce1c8713-2cf3-5d3e-be72-a3135dfaed78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1,\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402690} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5c07fd-aef8-50a6-bb7a-0214e75d1fbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=1,\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402691} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d397dd-0706-56c3-a545-4f1e11b5d1fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402692} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3acbb2-152b-5386-a5b3-d4d5f9778946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402693} {:answer/id #uuid "466aa8d5-5559-5bc5-8a5d-ddd22b7bb453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9d397dd-0706-56c3-a545-4f1e11b5d1fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402692} :answer-field/id #uuid "aba4d313-1d11-58c4-bb5b-3d3a12220a0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402689}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca4d349e-c1ee-5a19-a1e0-b0c9e7e02dd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-39793" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x}{(x+3)(x+1)(x-1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x+1)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the three solutions $x=-3,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are  $x=-3,\\,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087545 :question/id #uuid "011cf746-baa5-5395-8515-3beb98ff22a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5302" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x-2}{x^2-6x+8}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-6x+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-2}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x = 4.$ Therefore, $x = 4$ is the only vertical asymptote."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088165 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "890b8337-3605-5539-bf84-de88d0671ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085645} {:answer/id #uuid "bb07a5bd-4864-579b-b0a4-d2fa7187f553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085646} {:answer/id #uuid "3043a706-6fe7-524d-83d2-2b26cf05e66a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2, x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085647} {:answer/id #uuid "5aaf2864-92f8-5242-add4-be5b354019b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085648} {:answer/id #uuid "758788a8-b3b6-5d98-a458-d212cc992dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = ± 2, x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085649}] :answer-field/id #uuid "520f2ba8-beb6-5026-a9ab-92d74b99b0d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085644}] :question/id #uuid "e3bb1d12-e627-544f-8e0b-d96686104083" :question/math-academy-id "q-39800" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{x^{2} - 3x + 2}{x^{2} + x - 2}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8afbd978-e53e-5ea5-9498-b94770ceec43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085651} {:answer/id #uuid "be941d0f-6be5-5a24-b8f9-e6367f97c509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-4, x = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085652} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8ff229-44a7-532b-afcf-e1b73cbbbf06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2, x = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085653} {:answer/id #uuid "06c70408-8060-51a6-96f3-86a621554676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2, x = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085654} {:answer/id #uuid "5721fdb5-9b94-52f7-ab2a-82c030ace38a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085655}] :answer-field/id #uuid "409cf614-3a0c-5b15-863a-ce33a5c8e65c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085650}] :question/id #uuid "e2333ec7-70ae-546a-9712-c5a567a32656" :question/math-academy-id "q-39801" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{x - 2}{x^{2} + 2x - 8}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "814507ea-f563-57c6-ba16-848a48a57db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 0, x = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086970} {:answer/id #uuid "c35dd378-61e0-57d2-bf30-b547652ac1a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086971} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2ef318-0c0f-5ce1-9057-c9cd4b80dfee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086972} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7b4677-b5c3-51e5-95e5-657eb0a28e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086973} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec2efe8-b979-54cc-a27f-528cf8dbba1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2, x = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086974}] :answer-field/id #uuid "94a7e764-e67d-5112-9873-85a6e14dc106" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086969}] :question/id #uuid "38d33573-721b-5c42-99c6-39f5804f5848" :question/math-academy-id "q-80690" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = (x)/(x(x - 2))$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d24de3f8-0012-5833-9d24-f3e6cc246135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d24de3f8-0012-5833-9d24-f3e6cc246135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232487} :answer-field/id #uuid "847bb467-9bef-57c4-b13d-4741ccbfc6e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232486}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d09a218-37ee-56a7-9539-64f2a5b50504" :question/math-academy-id "q-278536" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707649a0-0233-500b-aace-11a761c5191e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "707649a0-0233-500b-aace-11a761c5191e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232489} :answer-field/id #uuid "eea98157-b5fc-5648-86c8-678280c80d9e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232488}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e1e56fe-2c76-5e2f-82a8-c8d18f84edaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-278543" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{2}+x-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{2}+x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)(x+2)}{(x-1)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{(x-1)\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-1=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234020 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9da44686-a271-5d67-9dd0-7fb4a1f7283c" :question/math-academy-id "q-39802" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-2}{{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-2}{{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+1)}{{x}^{2}(x+7)-(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+1)}{({x}^{2}-1)(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)\\cancel{(x+1)}}{(x-1)\\cancel{(x+1)}(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{(x-1)(x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x+7)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-7$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-7$ and $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cf4edc2-58db-5d46-80e0-a7c8624e48ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cf4edc2-58db-5d46-80e0-a7c8624e48ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290884} :answer-field/id #uuid "d228aeb4-71ac-53dc-81f5-63488c7179c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292371}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e4ca48a-2999-5392-a60c-be71cb24f7a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-269990" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+x-6}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+x-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{(x+3)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+3}}{\\cancel{(x+3)}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea1d276b-e77e-5cde-9521-ff30bc6339be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea1d276b-e77e-5cde-9521-ff30bc6339be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293845} :answer-field/id #uuid "399a722a-55f8-57cc-8ae2-0074bc503803" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294126}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aff4a32a-0112-57be-8fab-aa8ac02ff1a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-278535" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{(x+2)(\\cancel{x-1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx+2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc5bed91-b8c8-5871-b6be-0c61c8ab9057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc5bed91-b8c8-5871-b6be-0c61c8ab9057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293919} :answer-field/id #uuid "368f35c1-f002-5520-85cd-8a682f5c9611" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294117} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd432d19-3c99-5837-b724-f7d07317cf6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd432d19-3c99-5837-b724-f7d07317cf6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293671} :answer-field/id #uuid "440128d5-7483-59ac-865c-25e6bd35bb30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294169}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93ca9dc7-95c6-5564-8115-405f90e6185c" :question/math-academy-id "q-278545" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-4x+4}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-4x+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}(x-1)-4(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{({x}^{2}-4)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{(x-2)(x+2)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+2}}{(x-2)\\cancel{(x+2)}(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x-1)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $x=1$ and $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=1$ and $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295029 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90c5905a-5f25-523a-ac65-98f8c0df5881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90c5905a-5f25-523a-ac65-98f8c0df5881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293495} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec8ff405-12ed-5fc1-921e-7aa13337b0fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294759}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61a7812d-e909-52ca-b959-ce3ac589b203" :question/math-academy-id "q-278556" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-1}{2{x}^{2}+x-1}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x-1}{2{x}^{2}+x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-1}{(x+1)(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2x-1}}{(x+1)\\cancel{(2x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx+1=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07cecb66-e549-5c1c-ad09-78a83dd33eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324042} {:answer/id #uuid "55df2ba0-f2c6-5604-9a35-63997cf52830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324686} {:answer/id #uuid "cb249387-39bb-5bca-aeb9-bb3d6e5d3f07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325733} {:answer/id #uuid "e80c846d-e256-503b-bd6e-c02635e34775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326014} {:answer/id #uuid "f0231bbc-1648-5f25-943c-15ee288b5418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07cecb66-e549-5c1c-ad09-78a83dd33eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324042} :answer-field/id #uuid "75e143c6-8b95-56da-82eb-2acad7352c9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326431}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cd01e245-cc8a-5763-b4a7-8579762d92bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-39804" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+3x-1}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+3x-1}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1{)}^{3}}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x-1{)}^{2}}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x-1{)}^{2}}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1{)}^{2}}{(x+5{)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x+5{)}^{2}=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-5.$ Therefore, $x=-5$ is the only vertical asymptote."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2964b3dc-2d94-539a-946c-a30b2755e2da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324304} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6cc3a3-58f1-5d26-8673-5d478b6c9cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324724} {:answer/id #uuid "73ff3097-7a21-5a24-ac11-6c28413a6ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324935} {:answer/id #uuid "932a43ae-c4e3-506f-b284-5316e420c58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325221} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e97693-64f6-596d-9b4a-111e079d80af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "932a43ae-c4e3-506f-b284-5316e420c58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325221} :answer-field/id #uuid "76f771b5-672e-5778-ace6-2b8c22fa7988" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326435}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "720a20e0-eaeb-515c-b041-a17373ebe5f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39931" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{{x}^{2}(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{{x}^{2}\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is the only vertical asymptote."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e616195-c20c-5ce6-a774-a9d27760ce9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345468} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbadb36-36cf-57bb-b77c-60cf2f937db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346153} {:answer/id #uuid "91cba78e-fa38-55f5-8f5a-e2ea6d4d0101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346626} {:answer/id #uuid "a473e00b-5a25-5f34-8225-eb11ceb36777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346779} {:answer/id #uuid "e22e0eef-d46c-5409-9e99-5a2584e7abcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91cba78e-fa38-55f5-8f5a-e2ea6d4d0101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346626} :answer-field/id #uuid "2918e306-8491-5fc4-93b6-c33073f50077" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347671}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3dad69b3-29eb-58b2-9a73-eefa5740f30c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80691" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+2}}{x\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is the only vertical asymptote."}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087844 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087544 :question/id #uuid "f4879578-ac07-54f8-b00f-5ab651ae240a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5301" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{2}{x^3+x^2+4x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x({x}^{2}+x+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(x^2 +x+ 4) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x = 0$ gives the solution $x = 0.$\n\n- The equation $x^2 +x+ 4 = 0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(1{)}^{2}-4(1)(4) \\\\  & =1-16 \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x = 0.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24b3ce4f-f02a-5973-8510-abddd72abd3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324272} {:answer/id #uuid "27e02c0c-7e36-5287-8af8-5b938f095220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324290} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c52696-ba99-57ea-8f2e-c13d827516e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325525} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ff13b6-5212-585e-8824-169927916126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325815} {:answer/id #uuid "fa94debb-5a3f-5611-9395-ed887d33efbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,\\,x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27e02c0c-7e36-5287-8af8-5b938f095220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324290} :answer-field/id #uuid "40259e5d-4cd1-55c7-8aff-27f6b5a4499e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326324}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bac7c54b-040c-5d96-8fcc-40e2d221cff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-39795" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx({x}^{2}+x+1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $2x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+1=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d3a4c00-8634-58fc-90d6-3294ac3c3371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327471} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7e65d5-7788-5c01-8ad0-486d9b40f491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328172} {:answer/id #uuid "a11c362b-0329-5e15-9f0d-4bb67c042f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=9$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328331} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b8294a-f4a8-5a82-a733-0419b620a731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,x=0,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328625} {:answer/id #uuid "c933dda2-ec84-5062-ac4e-5f530086befd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e7e65d5-7788-5c01-8ad0-486d9b40f491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328172} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f8cee54-9108-570e-b47f-cbdac909560a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329209}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "683930f5-7231-57ef-90a9-236b0f94178e" :question/math-academy-id "q-4694" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-27}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-27} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+9)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the difference of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+9)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $(x-3)=0$ gives the solution $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+3x+9=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-4(1)(9) \\\\  & =9-36 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fe654d7-6432-5cc0-b1f6-df2c2b01d27b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345899} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb89589-5f3e-556f-8392-084981699059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346024} {:answer/id #uuid "778231e9-1503-5fe8-97dc-d51787729671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,\\,x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346401} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec274e4-80d6-5f09-bde5-9e5dd10197d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346465} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bf79fa-b327-5731-b2d4-26beca27d1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eb89589-5f3e-556f-8392-084981699059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346024} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8ede0ea-63a7-5cb0-bb81-4c437869eaec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348010}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90dc5317-bc84-5136-bb1e-99a7e0621f24" :question/math-academy-id "q-80679" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x+5=0.\n$$\n\nNote that the above equation has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-4{)}^{2}-4(1)(5) \\\\  & =16-20 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233171 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6959c63-587b-5482-a15e-ffcddfea0d62" :question/math-academy-id "q-113523" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+3x-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+3x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2)}{{x}^{2}(x-1)+3(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2)}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+3)(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-1=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+3=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233730 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffd9b367-054b-5790-ace7-f4b19449eb9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffd9b367-054b-5790-ace7-f4b19449eb9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232491} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ec6d923-f04a-5e6b-8731-9e140c7c04b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232490}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28775c75-5409-5ea8-bed7-941c85b2b6e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-278551" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{4}{2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-1}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2{x}^{2}(x-1)+(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(2{x}^{2}+1)(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-1=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+1=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234067 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c29f5a25-a27e-5aa7-83e3-1b45bff33614" :question/math-academy-id "q-4698" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{8{x}^{4}+x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{8{x}^{4}+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x(8{x}^{3}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x[(2x)^{3}+{1}^{3}]} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x(2x+1)(4{x}^{2}-2x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the sum of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(2x+1)(4{x}^{2}-2x+1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- Solving $2x+1=0$ gives the solution $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}-2x+1=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-4(4)(1) \\\\  & =4-16 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=0$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234422 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93c6c7f3-46fa-5107-aed4-196cf2a60bfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-80680" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+3x+4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nNote that the equation ${x}^{2}+3x+4=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-4(1)(4) \\\\  & =9-16 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d506aa-841e-5c5b-a1bf-5ac09f60017e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244603} {:answer/id #uuid "462c3d52-8b72-513b-980e-c15197879d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244940} {:answer/id #uuid "65c64b8c-1872-5671-805d-e70b0c6511d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245211} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1ce093-9dd6-5b3f-aef8-928fca896ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-7$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245542} {:answer/id #uuid "ede102f7-ea3c-59b6-a3e1-e4bb8edb3ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "462c3d52-8b72-513b-980e-c15197879d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244940} :answer-field/id #uuid "f83b8a24-f8de-5be9-ab19-5920066dd724" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246989}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "163eeae3-71ad-5b66-a0aa-4b05d346c12b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113525" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x({x}^{2}+x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx({x}^{2}+x+7)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+7=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(7) \\\\  & =1-28 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c01eca7f-16b5-518e-b8bd-7e734e5b4985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c01eca7f-16b5-518e-b8bd-7e734e5b4985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291460} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a220a84-d2be-57bd-97d7-693834b3b0b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292009}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6d17235-f50b-5069-894a-be17656793ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-270003" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+5x-20}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+5x-20} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}(x-4)+5(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-3}{({x}^{2}+5)(x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+5)(x-4)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-4=0$ gives the solution $x=4.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+5=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d05da919-52fc-509f-a8f8-2528fc830fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d05da919-52fc-509f-a8f8-2528fc830fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291517} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd09b302-f2ca-548f-87d3-3d4055d98ae4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292519}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b59dc04-b4de-517d-b908-41d7f801e2fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-270004" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2{x}^{3}-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2{x}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2({x}^{3}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{2({x}^{3}-{1}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{2(x-1)({x}^{2}+x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the difference of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(x-1)({x}^{2}+x+1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $(x-1)=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+1=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102836a1-9ad7-5910-882e-215f9e14dbcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333137} {:answer/id #uuid "31b9e16b-6bcb-5bbb-9138-174047d6690f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333445} {:answer/id #uuid "a33ef2aa-239f-5c75-8c30-e1d0699850fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334423} {:answer/id #uuid "e3664c3d-5247-52ed-824d-0d47b62cf94b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334969} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ae6dd4-cd83-5007-ae93-ff5a0f083e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8ae6dd4-cd83-5007-ae93-ff5a0f083e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335168} :answer-field/id #uuid "8004c65d-a62f-59aa-85b4-bdb65ced464c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335484}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3613299d-7197-59d6-84af-a80c57279c37" :question/math-academy-id "q-6912" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{3{x}^{2}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31037d7d-615f-586a-8606-01f1b4558592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345784} {:answer/id #uuid "4aeb91f0-cd53-5d4d-886c-fb0fd62c216e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345993} {:answer/id #uuid "72812abe-a3ca-5de6-b805-36c4e38ad777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346366} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe36be7-496a-56d8-8d43-bee44a476d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346750} {:answer/id #uuid "e47b15d6-db10-5d9f-9cc1-04b2bdf4db96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31037d7d-615f-586a-8606-01f1b4558592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345784} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d9c99d4-e1f0-5d10-83b9-846a389a4e58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347736}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e336039-4f92-550b-b76c-a2a62662e884" :question/math-academy-id "q-80676" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+4x-8}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+4x-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}(x-2)+4(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-1}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+4)(x-2)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-2=0$ gives the solution $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+4=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399795 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a12e93a-5479-5bc8-a172-5f001b81680d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399797} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd56062-f5eb-5d51-97c9-2401cc2382c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399798} {:answer/id #uuid "50434bd2-8dc8-5df0-b8fa-4d7fa1f9bf18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399799} {:answer/id #uuid "060d6765-1a4c-5cef-9036-baa44df95b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399800} {:answer/id #uuid "0532888d-2587-5d82-aa30-da64f92b3ef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50434bd2-8dc8-5df0-b8fa-4d7fa1f9bf18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399799} :answer-field/id #uuid "9de2cff7-92c8-5c70-9102-18ed3f31a5ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399796}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "046b8096-a4b3-5639-81fd-05cfaf3fcc14" :question/math-academy-id "q-113527" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+6x-12}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+6x-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-2)+6(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-3)}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+6)(x-2)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-2=0$ gives the solution $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+6=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}]}
